1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,800
How much of your WooCommerce revenue comes from <b>returning customers</b>?

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,700
This is a WooCommerce demo store running PureDevs Customer History.

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:12,000
Open <b>Customer History</b>, <b>Dashboard</b>, and scroll to Revenue Analytics.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,200
This is a <b>Pro report</b>.

5
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:17,500
It's self-hosted — <b>nothing leaves your server</b>.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,000
It reads your store's own orders.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,400
Here's the split.

8
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:22,400
<b>New</b> Customers.

9
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:23,400
<b>Returning</b> Customers.

10
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:24,400
<b>Guest</b> Orders.

11
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:29,300
Each card shows the revenue, and the orders behind it.

12
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:35,000
New and Returning count customers <b>with an account</b>; guest orders are placed without one.

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
One date range, three cards.

14
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:44,400
WooCommerce's own Orders report can show orders from new or returning customers, one filter at a time.

15
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:54,000
Customer History adds new, returning and guest revenue <b>side by side</b> for the <b>same dates</b>, so you can decide where your next hour goes.

16
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,500
Now <b>read the proportion, not the number</b>.

17
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,500
Returning Customers is the biggest of the three.

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
And guests? The smallest card.

19
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,300
So here is <b>the decision</b> this gives you.

20
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,500
Guests are small here.

21
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,000
If yours is big, decide whether to encourage <b>accounts at checkout</b> — repeat guests never show as returning.

22
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,700
And look at the biggest card.

23
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:28,500
Returning is the biggest — so <b>your next hour</b> belongs to the customers who already buy from you.

24
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:33,000
That's where the <b>biggest share of this revenue</b> comes from.

25
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,600
The plugin doesn't decide for you.

26
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:39,000
It shows you <b>where the money comes from</b>.

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
The <b>free version</b> shows your repeat purchase rate.

28
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,600
This split is Pro.

29
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:46,000
The link below <b>starts the trial</b>.
